ng Miami GOP support", "Rodriguez's Red to Blue status signals national Democratic investment"], "rationale": "The fundamentals still favor Salazar: an R+6 seat, a large fundraising edge, and most raters calling the race Lean or Likely R. But recent polls show a tie or one-point Democratic edge, Trump's attacks create an unusual incumbent-loyalty problem, and Democratic outside investment justifies nudging the expected margin to about R+2.5.", "watch": "Whether Trump's feud with Salazar depresses Republican turnout in Miami-Dade, or whether her incumbency and funding advantage reassert themselves in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Representative María Elvira Salazar seeks a third term in a Miami-Dade district that leans Republican by about six points, facing former news anchor Eliott Rodriguez. Salazar holds a large financial advantage and has racked up endorsements, including from María Corina Machado and the Everglades Trust, while advertising her federal funding wins.\n\nStill, the race has tightened: recent surveys show the candidates even, Rodriguez has joined the DCCC's Red to Blue program, and Trump has publicly attacked Salazar over her criticism of his immigration crackdown, an unusual rift in a heavily Republican-leaning Cuban American seat. Most forecasters still rate the race Lean R, with markets giving Salazar roughly a two-in-three chance.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2129, "margin": -4.43}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2851, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2818, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3773, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2766, "margin": -3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3218, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3331, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3785, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3582, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "